From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 18:06:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(conpty): never inject our own sequence inside the
 application's (KD-26)

A full-screen frame written over ConPTY arrives mangled about half the
time: the frame lands a row low with stale rows beneath it, and tails of
escape sequences are printed as literal text - `;240m`, `[95m`, `291X`.
Reported from use; reproduces on both engines and not on the terminal the
Store ships, and swapping only OpenConsole.exe between two otherwise
identical installs moves the fault.

In ConPTY mode WriteCharsVT parses the application's output into the
buffer *and* passes the original bytes through verbatim. The parser
tolerates a write that ends mid-sequence; the writer did not know the
question existed. Anything conhost emits of its own - a CUP, the cursor
re-sync's DSR - was appended to the same buffer regardless, landing
between the head and the tail of a sequence the application was still
writing. The terminal then sees a complete CSI inside an incomplete one:
the inner ESC aborts the sequence in progress, the injected sequence is
dispatched, and the rest of the application's arrives as text.

Writes are split at arbitrary offsets in normal operation - conhost's own
parser comments on WSL delivering 16 byte chunks - so which sequence gets
cut, and where, varies run to run. That is the 50%.

VtIo now follows the application's bytes through their sequences
(_trackPassthrough: ground, escape, CSI, and the string sequences ended
by BEL or ST), and everything conhost generates goes through _ownSink(),
which returns the live buffer at a boundary and a pending buffer
mid-sequence. Pending bytes are appended the moment the application's
sequence completes, so ours are delayed rather than misplaced.
WritePassthrough marks which writes carry the application's bytes, since
that is the only path that can leave the stream mid-sequence.
WriteCharsLegacy is untouched: control characters are stripped or handled
individually there, so no sequence survives to be split.

Bisected to upstream da0446a7d ("Send a CPR request on every unknown
sequence", #20009) - eight rounds, unpatched upstream conhost built per
step with a fixed toolchain, swapping only OpenConsole.exe, 20 replays
per verdict. That commit did not add the injection; the machinery
predates it. It widened the trigger from resizes to every unknown
sequence, and the latch is sticky, which turned a latent race constant.
The tests below show the defect without that path at all.

Three tests, each checked in both directions:

    NoInjectionInsideAnIncompleteSequence         fails before, passes after
    NoInjectionInsideAnIncompleteStringSequence   fails before, passes after
    OwnWritesAreNotHeldBackBetweenSequences       passes before and after

The third is the guard against over-correcting: waiting for a sequence to
finish must not become withholding our own writes when there is nothing
to wait for. VtIoTests 16 -> 18/18.

ScreenBufferTests::ResizeCursorUnchanged aborts the test host at
assert(gci.IsConsoleLocked()) in SetConptyCursorPositionMayBeWrong. It
does so with this change stashed too - pre-existing upstream, verified,
not ours.

